# Code Style Rules

These rules make functions easy to read for a new team member: every function
says *why* it exists, and each body block states its *goal* and *why*.

## 0. Clarity (applies to every comment)

Every comment — function-level or block-level — must state the purpose clearly.

- Be concrete, name the actual target. Use the real subject (e.g. "product
  display", "deal_skus") instead of vague words like "anywhere", "anything",
  "somewhere", "stuff", "handle", or "process".
- The reader should grasp the purpose from the comment alone, without reading the
  code for context.
- Readable over compressed. A new teammate should understand the comment on the
  first read. Do not pack several ideas into a jargon phrase (e.g.
  "min-priority-per-key set"). Write a full sentence that names the subject, the
  rule, and the outcome.
- Short is not the goal; clear is. Prefer one extra line over a comment the
  reader has to decode.

## 1. Function comment (the "why")

Every function has a comment directly above it that explains the business logic.

- The first line is a summary of *why* the function exists / what it
  guarantees — not a line-by-line description of *how*. Use two sentences when
  one would be cryptic.
- If edge cases or multiple guarantees need explaining, list them as bullets
  (`// - Verify ...`) so the idea is easy to scan. Prefer a list over a
  paragraph.
- Each bullet is a full, self-contained sentence (subject + rule + outcome),
  not a compressed fragment. Follow the clarity rule in section 0.
- Do not restate the signature (parameter/return types are already visible).
- Start with the function name (Go doc-comment convention).

Good (preferred one-liner):

```go
// verifyBannerNotStored checks no product display is associated with a banner-only landing page.
func (l *landingPageUsecase) verifyBannerNotStored(...) { ... }
```

Good (summary + scannable list when there are several checks):

```go
// validateLandingPage validates one landing page. It verifies:
// - data is stored correctly according to the landing page template
// - stored deal_skus match the skus resolved from deal_ids
func (l *landingPageUsecase) validateLandingPage(...) { ... }
```

Bad (how — narrates the implementation):

```go
// This function takes a ctx, a reporter and a landingPage and calls the repository
// then loops over the result and appends to storedSKUs and adds an issue.
func (l *landingPageUsecase) verifyBannerNotStored(...) { ... }
```

Bad (compressed — a new teammate has to decode the jargon):

```go
// - default_sku and deal_default_sku both carry the same min-priority-per-key set
```

Good (same idea, readable):

```go
// - For each pin key, keep the smallest priority among all children (highest precedence).
// - Both default_sku and deal_default_sku get this same list so listing-page and flash-deal sort share one pin order.
```

## 2. Function body: labelled logical blocks

Split the body into logical blocks separated by a single blank line. Each block
does one conceptual step of the business logic.

- Put a short comment at the top of each block with **goal** and **why**:
  - **goal**: the outcome this step is trying to achieve, named concretely
  - **why**: the business reason, so the reader has context without reading the
    code
- Prefer one line when both fit without becoming cryptic: `// <goal> so <why>.`
- Use two lines when packing them into one would force jargon or omit context —
  goal first, why second. Follow the clarity rule in section 0.
- Do not narrate each statement (no "loop X then copy Y then assign Z"). The
  goal names the outcome, not the mechanics.
- A block should be a meaningful unit of work, not a single trivial line. Group
  the statements that together accomplish one step.
- Keep the natural order of the domain flow. For example, validating a landing
  page reads as: get landing page / pre-process -> extract deal skus -> verify
  against product displays.
- Error handling that belongs to a step stays inside that step's block.

Good (one line — goal + why):

```go
// Copy parent_pins onto default_sku so listing-page sort is not limited to the deal representative.
if defaultSKU != "" && defaultSKU != dealDefaultSKU {
```

Good (two lines when the why needs the edge):

```go
// Copy parent_pins onto default_sku when it is not the deal representative.
// Listing-page sort needs the normal representative, not only deal_default_sku.
```

Bad (why only — the reader still has to infer what the block does):

```go
// Listing-page sort reads parent_pins on default_sku, which may not be the deal representative.
```

Bad (goal only — no business context):

```go
// Copy parent_pins onto default_sku.
```

Bad (how — narrates the next statements):

```go
// Make a copied slice and assign parentPins[defaultSKU].
```

Example shape:

```go
// validateLandingPage validates one landing page. It verifies:
// - data is stored correctly according to the landing page template
// - stored deal_skus match the skus resolved from deal_ids
func (l *landingPageUsecase) validateLandingPage(ctx context.Context, reporter *entity.Reporter, landingPage *entity.LandingPage) {
	// Skip product-display storage so banner-only landing pages are not listed as products.
	if landingPage.Template == entity.BannerTemplate {
		l.verifyBannerNotStored(ctx, reporter, landingPage)
		return
	}

	// Resolve deal_ids to skus so stored deal_skus can be verified against the source of truth.
	mapDealIDToSKUs, err := l.mapSkusToDealIDs(ctx, landingPage)
	if err != nil {
		reporter.Add(entity.LandingPageIssue{
			LandingPageID: landingPage.ID,
			ScheduleID:    defaultScheduleID,
			Type:          issueTypeError,
			Message:       "failed to map skus to deal ids: " + err.Error(),
		})
		return
	}

	// Verify stored deal_skus vs resolved skus so every schedule matches the source of truth.
	l.verifyDealSKUs(reporter, landingPage.ID, defaultScheduleID, landingPage.DealIDs, landingPage.DealSkus, mapDealIDToSKUs)
	for _, schedule := range landingPage.Schedules {
		l.verifyDealSKUs(reporter, landingPage.ID, schedule.ID, schedule.DealIDs, schedule.DealSkus, mapDealIDToSKUs)
	}

	// Verify membership and pinned positions so the landing page is materialized in product displays.
	l.verifyStoredInProductDisplays(ctx, reporter, landingPage, mapDealIDToSKUs)
}
```

## 3. When to relax the rules

- Very small functions (a guard, a one-line getter, a trivial constructor) do not
  need internal blocks; the function comment alone is enough.
- If a step is genuinely a single self-explanatory line, a block comment can be
  omitted — do not add noise just to satisfy the format.
- Never split a block so finely that each block is one line; blocks must carry a
  complete step of logic.
